    description: "Interactive 3D thermodynamic heat-engine apparatus featuring a high-temperature Hot Reservoir (T_H), a low-temperature Cold Reservoir (T_C), a cutaway working-substance cylinder and reciprocating piston, Heat-Input (Q_H) and Heat-Rejection (Q_C) energy-flow arrows, a rotating mechanical Work-Output flywheel shaft (W), and a synchronized 3D Pressure\u2013Volume (P\u2013V) cycle diagram.",
    whatIsIt: "A 3D thermodynamic heat-engine laboratory demonstrating how a cyclic engine absorbs heat Q_H from a hot reservoir, converts part of it into mechanical work W, rejects waste heat Q_C to a cold reservoir, and obeys the Carnot efficiency limit.",
    howDoesItWork: "A heat engine operates cyclically between a Hot Reservoir at absolute temperature T_H (in Kelvin) and a Cold Reservoir at T_C (where T_H > T_C). Across four reversible Carnot stages\u2014(1\u21922) Isothermal Expansion at T_H absorbing heat Q_H, (2\u21923) Adiabatic Expansion cooling from T_H to T_C, (3\u21924) Isothermal Compression at T_C rejecting heat Q_C, and (4\u21921) Adiabatic Compression heating back to T_H\u2014the working gas returns to its initial state (\u0394U_cycle = 0). By the First Law of Thermodynamics, W = Q_H - Q_C. By the Second Law of Thermodynamics, some heat Q_C > 0 must always be rejected to a colder reservoir, limiting the maximum possible efficiency to the Carnot efficiency \u03B7_Carnot = 1 - T_C / T_H.",
