    concept: "Einstein's Photon Quantum E = hf, Work Function \u03C6, Threshold Frequency f\u2080 & Stopping Potential",
    description: "Interactive 3D quantum photoelectric apparatus featuring a tunable monochromatic light source, quantized photon wave-packets, an evacuated quartz tube with a photosensitive metal target cathode, collector anode, emitted photoelectrons (strictly gated by hf \u2265 \u03C6), external ammeter/voltmeter circuit, stopping-potential indicator, and a 3D quantum energy-level diagram.",
    whatIsIt: "A 3D quantum-physics laboratory demonstrating Einstein\u2019s photoelectric effect: individual photons of energy E = hf eject electrons from a metal surface only when the photon frequency exceeds the metal\u2019s threshold frequency (hf \u2265 \u03C6).",
    howDoesItWork: "Light consists of discrete energy quanta (photons) each carrying energy E_photon = hf, where h is Planck\u2019s constant and f is light frequency. A metal binds conduction electrons with a minimum escape energy called the work function \u03C6. In the standard one-photon photoelectric effect, if hf < \u03C6 (below threshold frequency f\u2080 = \u03C6/h), NO electrons are emitted regardless of how bright (intense) the light is. When hf \u2265 \u03C6 (above threshold), electrons are ejected immediately with maximum kinetic energy K_max = hf - \u03C6. Increasing light frequency f increases K_max and the stopping potential V_stop = K_max / e; increasing light intensity at fixed frequency above threshold increases the photon flux (number of photons per second) and photoelectron emission rate (photocurrent), without changing K_max.",
    realLifeExample: "Solar photovoltaic cells, digital camera CMOS/CCD image sensors, night-vision photomultiplier tubes, automatic doors, and photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS).",
    examConnection: "Einstein\u2019s Photoelectric Equation: E_photon = hf = hc/\u03BB, and K_max = hf - \u03C6 = e\xB7V_stop (for hf \u2265 \u03C6). Threshold frequency: f\u2080 = \u03C6/h. Below f\u2080, zero emission at any intensity. Above f\u2080, frequency controls K_max while intensity controls photocurrent.",
