    description: "Interactive 3D geometrical optics bench featuring converging (convex) and diverging (concave) lenses, movable luminous object arrow, principal axis, focal markers (F, 2F), three color-coded principal rays, real/virtual image formation, and microscope/telescope configurations.",
    whatIsIt: "A precision 3D geometrical optics laboratory that traces principal light rays through converging and diverging lenses and multi-lens optical instruments.",
    howDoesItWork: "Using the Cartesian thin-lens sign convention\u2014where light travels left-to-right, the object is placed to the left of the lens at negative coordinate u = \u2212|u| < 0, a converging (biconvex) lens has f > 0, and a diverging (biconcave) lens has f < 0\u2014the image coordinate v satisfies the thin-lens equation 1/f = 1/v \u2212 1/u (so v = f\xB7u / (f + u)), and lateral magnification is m = v/u = h_i/h_o. When |u| > f for a converging lens, refracted rays physically intersect on the right (v > 0) to form an inverted real image (m < 0). When |u| < f for a converging lens, or for any object distance with a diverging lens, refracted rays diverge on the right and their backward dashed extensions intersect on the left (v < 0) to form an upright virtual image (m > 0).",
    realLifeExample: "Camera objectives and cinema projectors forming real images on sensors/screens; magnifying glasses, corrective eyeglasses, compound microscopes, and astronomical refracting telescopes.",
