    concept: "Space-Charge Depletion Zone, Built-In Potential Barrier, Energy Band Bending & Shockley Diode I\u2013V Law",
    description: "Interactive 3D solid-state semiconductor laboratory showing a doped p\u2013n junction crystal, uncovered acceptor (\u2212) and donor (+) ions in the depletion region, mobile holes (h\u207A) and electrons (e\u207B), 3D conduction/valence energy bands (E_c, E_v, E_g), and the exponential Shockley I\u2013V curve.",
    whatIsIt: "A 3D solid-state physics explorer demonstrating how a p\u2013n semiconductor junction rectifies electric current by modulating its depletion width W and potential barrier q(V_bi \u2212 V_bias).",
    howDoesItWork: "When p-type (majority holes h\u207A) and n-type (majority electrons e\u207B) semiconductors meet, carrier diffusion across the interface uncovers fixed negatively charged acceptor ions (N_A\u207B) on the p-side and positively charged donor ions (N_D\u207A) on the n-side. This space-charge depletion layer establishes an internal electric field and built-in potential V_bi (\u22480.70 V in Si, \u22480.30 V in Ge, \u22481.20 V in GaAs). Applying forward bias (V_bias > 0) opposes the internal field, narrows the depletion width W(V) = W\u2080\u221A(1 \u2212 V_bias/V_bi), lowers the energy band step q(V_bi \u2212 V_bias), and drives exponential current I_D = I_s(exp(qV_bias/(nk_BT)) \u2212 1). Applying reverse bias (V_bias < 0) widens W, raises the potential barrier, and blocks current.",
    realLifeExample: "Rectifier diodes in AC-to-DC power adapters; Light-Emitting Diodes (LEDs); photovoltaic solar cells; CMOS computer processor transistors.",
    examConnection: "Shockley Diode Equation: I_D = I_s (e^(qV / nk_BT) \u2212 1), where thermal voltage V_T = k_BT/q \u2248 25.85 mV at 300 K. Depletion width: W \u221D \u221A(V_bi \u2212 V_bias). Band gap energies: Ge (0.66 eV), Si (1.12 eV), GaAs (1.42 eV).",
