    description: "Interactive 3D molecular polarity laboratory for H\u2082O, CO\u2082, NH\u2083, CH\u2084, and BF\u2083 demonstrating how individual bond dipoles, partial atomic charges (\u03B4\u207A/\u03B4\u207B), lone-pair VSEPR geometry, and 3D vector addition determine net molecular polarity.",
    whatIsIt: "A 3D vector-addition and VSEPR molecular geometry laboratory that distinguishes bond polarity from overall molecular polarity.",
    howDoesItWork: "When two covalently bonded atoms differ in Pauling electronegativity (\u0394\u03C7 = |\u03C7_B \u2212 \u03C7_A|), shared bonding electrons shift toward the more electronegative atom, creating partial charges (\u03B4\u207A on the less electronegative atom, \u03B4\u207B on the more electronegative atom) and an individual bond dipole vector \u03BC_i (+\u2014\u25B8 pointing from \u03B4\u207A to \u03B4\u207B). However, polar bonds do NOT automatically make the entire molecule polar! Overall molecular polarity is the 3D vector sum \u03BC_net = \u03A3 \u03BC_i across the molecule\u2019s equilibrium VSEPR geometry. In linear CO\u2082 (180\xB0), trigonal planar BF\u2083 (120\xB0), and tetrahedral CH\u2084 (109.5\xB0), symmetrical arrangement causes the bond-dipole vectors to cancel completely (\u03BC_net = 0 D, NONPOLAR). In bent H\u2082O (104.5\xB0) and trigonal pyramidal NH\u2083 (107.0\xB0), lone-pair asymmetry prevents cancellation, yielding a strong resultant molecular dipole (\u03BC_net = 1.85 D for H\u2082O and 1.47 D for NH\u2083, POLAR).",
