    description: "Interactive 3D intermolecular attractions laboratory comparing Hydrogen Bonding (H\u2082O\u22EFH\u2082O), Dipole\u2013Dipole Interactions (HCl\u22EFHCl), and London Dispersion Forces (Ar\u22EFAr and I\u2082\u22EFI\u2082) with real-time distance & orientation controls, dashed non-covalent force overlays, thermal motion, and boiling-point comparison.",
    whatIsIt: "A 3D physical-chemistry laboratory showing how non-covalent electrostatic attractions arise between molecules and govern phase behavior.",
    howDoesItWork: "Intermolecular forces (IMFs) are non-covalent electrostatic attractions between separate molecules\u2014depicted as dashed force overlays rather than solid covalent bonds. (1) London Dispersion Forces arise from quantum fluctuations in electron clouds that create an instantaneous dipole in one molecule and induce a synchronized dipole in a neighbor (V \u221D \u2212\u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082/r\u2076); dispersion forces act between ALL atoms and molecules, and scale strongly with electron count and polarizability \u03B1. (2) Dipole\u2013Dipole Interactions occur between polar molecules with permanent partial charges (such as HCl\u22EFHCl) and depend strongly on relative orientation \u03B8 (attractive when \u03B4\u207A aligns with \u03B4\u207B, repulsive if like charges face). (3) Hydrogen Bonding occurs when H covalently bonded to N, O, or F interacts directionally with a lone pair on a neighboring N, O, or F atom (such as H\u2082O\u22EFH\u2082O). Notably, one IMF type is not always strongest in every comparison: large nonpolar I\u2082 (106 e\u207B, BP +184.3\xB0C) has stronger total dispersion forces than small polar HCl (18 e\u207B, BP \u221285.1\xB0C).",
    realLifeExample: "Explains why water boils at +100\xB0C (extensive hydrogen-bond network) while H\u2082S is a gas (\u221260\xB0C), and why halogens progress from gas (F\u2082, Cl\u2082) to liquid (Br\u2082) to solid (I\u2082) as polarizability \u03B1 increases.",
    examConnection: "Dispersion energy: V_disp \u221D \u2212\u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082/r\u2076 (present in ALL molecules). Keesom/Dipole\u2013Dipole energy: V_dip \u221D \u2212(\u03BC\u2081\u03BC\u2082/r\xB3)\xB7f(\u03B8). Hydrogen bond requirement: Donor D\u2013H (D = N, O, F) \u22EF :A Acceptor (A = N, O, F). Stronger net IMFs \u2794 higher normal boiling point and enthalpy of vaporization \u0394H_vap.",
