    concept: "Galvanic (Voltaic) Cells, Half-Cell Redox Potentials, Salt Bridge Charge Balance & Nernst Equation",
    description: "Interactive 3D galvanic-cell laboratory featuring Zinc\u2013Copper (Daniell), Copper\u2013Silver, and Iron\u2013Copper cells with separate half-cell beakers, KNO\u2083 salt bridge, external wire circuit, digital voltmeter, live Nernst equation calculator, and separate external electron conduction vs internal ion migration.",
    whatIsIt: "A functional 3D electrochemistry laboratory demonstrating how a spontaneous redox reaction generates electrical voltage across separated half-cells.",
    howDoesItWork: "In a standard Zinc\u2013Copper galvanic cell (Zn(s) | Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) || Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) | Cu(s)), oxidation occurs spontaneously at the negative Zinc Anode: Zn(s) \u2192 Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2e\u207B (E\xB0_anode = \u22120.76 V), while reduction occurs at the positive Copper Cathode: Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2e\u207B \u2192 Cu(s) (E\xB0_cathode = +0.34 V), yielding overall reaction Zn(s) + Cu\xB2\u207A(aq) \u2192 Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) + Cu(s). Electrons flow EXCLUSIVELY through the External Conducting Wire Circuit from the zinc anode (\u2212) through the voltmeter to the copper cathode (+); electrons NEVER travel through the salt bridge or electrolyte! Instead, the KNO\u2083 Salt Bridge maintains electrical neutrality by migrating NO\u2083\u207B anions into the anode half-cell and K\u207A cations into the cathode half-cell. At standard conditions (1.00 M, 298 K), E\xB0_cell = E\xB0_cathode \u2212 E\xB0_anode = (+0.34 V) \u2212 (\u22120.76 V) = +1.10 V. When concentrations vary from 1.00 M, live cell voltage follows the Nernst equation: E_cell = E\xB0_cell \u2212 (RT/nF) ln(Q).",
    realLifeExample: "Forms the fundamental basis of all chemical batteries, including alkaline cells, automotive lead-acid batteries, and lithium-ion energy storage.",
    examConnection: "Standard potential: E\xB0_cell = E\xB0_cathode \u2212 E\xB0_anode (+1.10 V for Zn\u2013Cu). Nernst equation: E_cell = E\xB0_cell \u2212 (RT / nF) ln(Q) where Q = [Anode Ion] / [Cathode Ion]. Free energy: \u0394G = \u2212nFE_cell. Anode = Oxidation (\u2212 in galvanic); Cathode = Reduction (+ in galvanic).",
